    "alert_id": "P006_VL_MONITORING_TIMING",
    "title": "Viral load testing earlier than guideline schedule",
    "message": "Guidelines state the first viral load for adults should be done 6 months after ART initiation, then every 6-12 months or when clinically indicated. This patient initiated ART 10 weeks ago, so a VL of 89 copies/mL today falls outside the scheduled monitoring interval. The result is reassuring, showing decline from 125,000 copies/mL, but a repeat VL at the 6-month mark is still required to confirm sustained suppression. No action is needed on the current result itself.",
    "issue_type": "monitoring_gap",
    "severity": "low",
    "recommended_action": "Schedule repeat viral load at 6 months after ART initiation, then every 6-12 months per guideline.",
    "citations": [
      {"page_number": 50, "chunk_id": "hiv_guidelines_art.extraction__hiv_guidelines_art.extraction__p50_c2"},
      {"page_number": 12, "chunk_id": "hiv_guidelines_art.extraction__hiv_guidelines_art.extraction__p12_c3"}
    ]
  },
  {
    "alert_id": "P006_ANC_VL_PLAN",
    "title": "No clinician plan documented for pregnant patient",
    "message": "Guidelines specify that pregnant women already on DTG-based regimens should be maintained on the regimen, with viral load monitored at 6 months after initiation and every 6-12 months thereafter. This 22-week pregnant patient on TDF/3TC/DTG has no assessment or plan documented for today's visit. The regimen itself is appropriate for pregnancy per guidelines. However, the absence of a documented plan creates a risk that required monitoring, including VL at 6 months and renal function follow-up, is not scheduled.",
    "issue_type": "plan_review",
    "severity": "moderate",
    "recommended_action": "Document a plan confirming continuation of TLD, repeat VL at 6 months on ART, and renal function monitoring given the creatinine rise.",
